\section{Algorithms and computational evidence}

\subsection{Component exploration}
Given a finite window $[-N,N]^2$ of cells and a placement restricted to it, the component of
a cell is computed by breadth-first search, testing adjacency by the squared-distance
criterion. By Lemma~\ref{lem:gridadj}, for $R\le1$ it suffices to test the eight
grid-neighbours of a cell, and in general only cells within $\lceil R\rceil+1$ in each
coordinate. The complexity is $O(N^2\lceil R\rceil^2)$ distance evaluations, all in exact
squared arithmetic.

\subsection{The periodic-path optimisation}
The natural attack on the conjecture $R_{\min}=\tfrac12$ restricts attention to
\emph{periodic drifting paths}: cell sequences $c_0,\dots,c_T$ with $c_T=c_0+(a,b)$ for
nonzero drift $(a,b)$, together with points $p_t$ in the corresponding cells minimising the
longest edge $\Lambda=\max_{0\le t<T}\|p_{t+1}-p_t\|$. Repeating the pattern gives a
bi-infinite chain percolating for $R>\Lambda$. For a fixed cell sequence the inner
minimisation is a convex second-order cone programme; the outer combinatorial problem grows
like $8^T$ before symmetry reduction. Exhaustive optimisation for $T\le4$ and randomised
search for $T=5,\dots,8$ never produced $\Lambda<\tfrac12$, the value $\tfrac12$ being
attained by the zig-zag of Definition~\ref{def:line} and its symmetric images.

\subsection{Adversarial cuts}
Straight horizontal cuts with horizontal stagger $s\in[0,1]$ give minimum crossing distance
$\sqrt{4+\min(s,1-s)^2}$, maximised at $s=\tfrac12$ with value $\tfrac{\sqrt{17}}2$.
Beating this requires a non-straight cut; no such improvement has been found.

\section{Discussion and open problems}

\begin{conjecture}[Sharp geometric threshold]\label{conj:half}
For every $R\le\tfrac12$ and every placement, all connected components of $G_R(C)$ are
finite; equivalently $R_{\min}=\tfrac12$.
\end{conjecture}

The heuristic is a slack recursion. Immediately after crossing $x=K$, the current point
satisfies $|x-K|<R$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:cross}. To advance one further column the path must
travel from within $R$ of $K$ to within $R$ of $K\pm1$, requiring horizontal displacement at
least $1-2R$ per column advance, giving $a_{t+1}\le a_t-(1-2R)$ for the signed slack
$a_t=x_t-K_t$. For $R<\tfrac12$ this drives the slack negative in finitely many steps. What
is missing is a joint potential in $x$ and $y$ controlling the recharging of slack by
direction reversals; the crude loose bound $2R$ used in Theorem~\ref{thm:confine} costs
exactly the factor separating $\tfrac13$ from $\tfrac12$. Conjecture~\ref{conj:half} is
falsifiable by one periodic drifting pattern with all edges strictly shorter than $\tfrac12$.

\begin{conjecture}[Quantitative subcriticality]
For every $R<\tfrac12$ there is $C(R)$ such that, for every placement, the component of any
cell has at most $C(R)$ cells, with $C(R)=O((1-2R)^{-2})$ as $R\uparrow\tfrac12$.
\end{conjecture}

Theorem~\ref{thm:confine} gives $C(R)=9$ for $R<\tfrac13$. The slack recursion suggests
diameter of order $(1-2R)^{-1}$ per coordinate, hence area $(1-2R)^{-2}$. In contrast with
Poisson percolation, where subcritical clusters are finite only almost surely, the bound here
is uniform over placements: a deterministic statement with no probability in it.

\begin{conjecture}[Sharp full connectivity]
For every $R<\sqrt5$ there is a placement whose Gilbert graph is disconnected; equivalently
$R_{\mathrm{full}}=\sqrt5$.
\end{conjecture}

The verified lower bound $\tfrac{\sqrt{17}}2$ comes from a straight staggered cut. Closing
the gap requires either a cleverer adversarial placement --- for instance a staircase cut
exploiting the $\sqrt5$ extremality of a single grid edge in both coordinates at once ---
or a proof that straight staggered cuts are extremal, which would instead give
$R_{\mathrm{full}}=\tfrac{\sqrt{17}}2$. The tension is that $\sqrt5$ is attained by a single
pair of cells ($p((0,0))=(0,0)$, $p((1,0))=(2,1)$), whereas disconnection requires an entire
cut to be uniformly long, and each point serves four neighbours at once.

\subsection*{The intermediate radius}
Least is known about $R_{\mathrm{conn}}$, where the bounds $\tfrac13\le R_{\mathrm{conn}}
\le1$ are far apart. The lower bound is inherited from percolation and is surely not sharp:
connecting all points is a much stronger demand. A natural guess is that the truth lies
strictly between $\tfrac12$ and $1$; constructing a placement connecting everything at a
range close to $1/\sqrt2$, by tilting the alignment so each point serves both a horizontal
and a vertical neighbour, is a concrete first target.

\subsection*{The random model}
The results above are placement-uniform and constrain the almost-sure threshold $R_c$ only
through $R_c\ge R_{\min}\ge\tfrac13$. The rigidity of the conditioning suggests that
Peierls-type contour arguments should work well: with exactly one point per cell, a dual
circuit of blocked cells has a combinatorial description with independent, explicitly
computable per-cell probabilities and no clustering to spoil the counting.

\subsection*{Applications}
Planned wireless networks (one base station per administrative cell, with siting freedom
inside it) make $R_{\mathrm{full}}$ the power guaranteeing connectivity regardless of siting
and $R_{\mathrm{conn}}$ the power achievable by ideal planning. Hyperuniform materials ---
photonic band-gap media, avian retinal cone mosaics, jammed packings --- have transport
governed by connectivity thresholds, and the constants $\tfrac12$, $1$, $\sqrt5$ benchmark a
maximally hyperuniform pattern. Finally, the confinement theorem is a worst-case design
guarantee: below range $\tfrac13$ of the cell width, no arrangement of one facility per cell
produces a service chain reaching beyond a $3\times3$ neighbourhood.

\section{Conclusion}

Conditioning Gilbert's disc model on the square lattice replaces one hard, unknown constant
with a family of sharp geometric ones. We established $\tfrac13\le R_{\min}\le\tfrac12$,
$\tfrac13\le R_{\mathrm{conn}}\le1$ and $\tfrac{\sqrt{17}}2\le R_{\mathrm{full}}\le\sqrt5$,
all uniformly over placements. The lower bound for $R_{\min}$ is a deterministic
non-percolation theorem --- for $R<\tfrac13$, every component of every placement fits in a
$3\times3$ block --- proved by a crossing lemma and a travelling invariant, and the upper
bounds are realised by three transparent extremal placements. The conjectures
$R_{\min}=\tfrac12$ and $R_{\mathrm{full}}=\sqrt5$ are each falsifiable by one explicit
example, and closing either gap appears to be within reach of a sufficiently clever
potential function.

